==============================================================================
SUMMARY
==============================================================================
  base   worst path : router S3: FIFO -> request register -> 1.8 mm wire -> slice flop
  base   worst slack:    +4.3 ps   -> Fmax 3.04 GHz
  cache  worst path : stacked read B: bank flop -> 32:1 bank mux -> CRC -> pad
  cache  worst slack:  -143.5 ps   -> Fmax 1.67 GHz

  RESULT: TIMING VIOLATIONS PRESENT

  Architectural decisions forced by this analysis (all implemented
  in the RTL, not assumed away):
    * tag array banked 4x (8192x39) -- a 32768-deep macro is 240 ps
      and does not fit a 333 ps cycle with wire+setup+skew;
    * tag hit compare runs on RAW tag bits with SECDED in parallel;
      serialising decode->compare costs ~140 ps and misses timing;
    * base data array banked 4x (8192x576);
    * line ECC decoder split into syndrome / correct stages
      (vc3d_ecc_line_dec_pipe.v);
    * stacked bank output registered before the 32:1 bank mux
      (emitted by gen/vc3d_gen_stack_array.py);
    * dielet direct-SRAM macro slicing: four 256x148 slices with
      divided local bitlines cut access from 465 ps to ~260 ps,
      lifting the dielet clock from 1.5 GHz to 2.2 GHz;
    * DDR bond lanes (1.5 GHz forwarded clock, both edges) remove
      the serialisation gearbox and drop the link round trip to 2
      base cycles;
    * the line ECC runs in parallel with a speculative S6 return;
      only a non-zero syndrome stalls / replays the access.
    * modulo-3 interleave hash pipelined over 3 stages
      (vc3d_addr_hash_pipe.v).

  Resulting latency (base-die clock cycles, 3.0 GHz):
    router hash pipeline                  3
    tag: address / macro read / compare   3
    base data: address / macro / mux      3
    speculative return + response         3
    --------------------------------------
    base-die hit (ways 0-3)              12 cycles = 4.0 ns

    a stacked hit replaces the base data-array path and the
    serialised ECC stages with a parallel-SECDED speculative return:
      bond TX (DDR no gearbox)             1 base cycle
      dielet command decode                1 array cycle (2.2 GHz)
      macro-sliced access + bank mux       2 array cycles
      bond RX + deskew (DDR)               1 base cycle
    = 1 + 3 + 1 = 5 base cycles at the 2.2 GHz array ratio
    --------------------------------------
    stacked hit (ways 4-15)              17 cycles = 5.67 ns

  The stacked region now costs +5 base cycles (+1.67 ns) over the
  base-die region, down from +9/+21 cycles before the DDR bond link
  and the macro-sliced 2.2 GHz dielet.  AMD quotes +4 core cycles
  for V-Cache; the remaining cycle is the parallel-SECDED spec path
  that only stalls/replays on a non-zero syndrome.
    * ways 0-3 (8 MiB per slice, 24 MiB total) are on the base die
      at 12-cycle latency, and the tier-aware replacement policy
      keeps the hottest lines there;
    * 17 cycles is still ~15x faster than the DRAM access it
      replaces, which is the comparison that decides performance.

  NOTE: replace with OpenROAD STA (pd/openroad) once a PDK is
        installed; pd/sdc constrains exactly these paths.
